1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,866
Three well-known results already touch what happens when models train on material that models made.

2
00:00:05,866 --> 00:00:11,642
Alignment faking asks whether a model defends its values when it thinks it is being retrained.

3
00:00:11,642 --> 00:00:16,336
Model collapse follows what degrades when generation after generation trains on model output.

4
00:00:16,336 --> 00:00:21,030
Attractor state work reports where two models settle when they talk long enough.

5
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:22,474
Each is a photograph.

6
00:00:22,474 --> 00:00:28,612
None of them follows one value through training, across settings and seeds, while it is still moving.

7
00:00:28,612 --> 00:00:31,500
That is the film I tried to shoot.

8
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:38,620
It matters now because a model's own answers already supply much of its next training data.

9
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:46,692
In this loop the model generates candidates, a selector keeps some, training folds those back in, and the successor replaces its predecessor.

10
00:00:46,692 --> 00:00:50,728
What the model values helps decide its own next training set.

11
00:00:51,478 --> 00:00:55,150
My loop is small enough to instrument at every stage.

12
00:00:55,150 --> 00:01:06,167
Each round the organism writes six candidate answers per prompt, a judge compares each against an alternative and keeps the two it prefers, and those two become the training data.

13
00:01:06,167 --> 00:01:08,371
Held-out prompts measure the value again.

14
00:01:08,371 --> 00:01:11,308
I varied the judge and both candidate sources.

15
00:01:12,058 --> 00:01:19,115
I fine-tuned two model families to hold a value, risk-seeking or insecure-code-generating, and the value runs zero to one.

16
00:01:19,115 --> 00:01:24,687
For the gambling organism it is the share of answers that pick the risky gamble.

17
00:01:24,687 --> 00:01:34,344
For the insecure-code organism it is how insecure its answers to three fixed questions about its own coding habits read, scored by its frozen base model.

18
00:01:35,094 --> 00:01:38,368
Before the runs played out I could not answer this.

19
00:01:38,368 --> 00:01:44,262
After round one I hold six candidates per prompt, the judge's scores on them, and one measured value.

20
00:01:44,262 --> 00:01:45,572
Four rounds are left.

21
00:01:45,572 --> 00:01:51,793
The baseline to beat is assuming nothing changes, which misses the final value by zero point four three one.

22
00:01:52,943 --> 00:01:55,146
I measure two things each round.

23
00:01:55,146 --> 00:02:01,755
Spread is the standard deviation of the candidates' value scores within a prompt, averaged over the round's prompts.

24
00:02:01,755 --> 00:02:06,162
Agreement is the correlation between the judge's scores and those value scores.

25
00:02:06,162 --> 00:02:14,974
Their product reconstructs the gap between the kept answers and the pool at R squared zero point eight zero across three hundred sixty-seven rounds.

26
00:02:15,724 --> 00:02:18,189
The one-round rule has no fitted coefficient.

27
00:02:18,189 --> 00:02:23,824
The next measured value is the mean value score of the two candidates the judge kept.

28
00:02:23,824 --> 00:02:36,151
Holding out each complete experimental condition, it predicts the next value with mean absolute error zero point zero eight one across three hundred forty rounds, against zero point one two eight for assuming no change.

29
00:02:36,901 --> 00:02:44,722
For endpoints I repeat that update from round one, with spread, agreement and pool composition held fixed and nothing re-measured.

30
00:02:44,722 --> 00:02:51,761
Endpoint error is zero point one one eight, against zero point four three one for assuming no change.

31
00:02:51,761 --> 00:02:59,973
The error grows slowly, zero point one zero zero one round out and zero point one three zero four rounds out.

32
00:03:01,123 --> 00:03:06,546
One predicted number per run is only the average path real runs scatter around.

33
00:03:06,546 --> 00:03:18,165
The stochastic version adds noise wherever the loop varies, in the value reading, the judge's picks, the training step and the drift in agreement, sized from the measured leftover errors.

34
00:03:18,165 --> 00:03:27,848
Sampled forward, simulated runs move about as much as observed ones, zero point seven zero nine of round-to-round change against zero point six four eight.

35
00:03:27,848 --> 00:03:32,883
Eighty-nine percent of observed final values land inside the model's eighty percent bands.

36
00:03:32,883 --> 00:03:39,855
The bands are wide, so the calibration holds over a population of runs, not for any one trajectory.

37
00:03:40,605 --> 00:03:43,522
The same two measurements say where to push.

38
00:03:43,522 --> 00:03:47,898
In one run the pool had collapsed and the value sat still.

39
00:03:47,898 --> 00:03:53,368
Mixing in base-model answers restored spread, and the judge's agreement then eroded the stuck value.

40
00:03:53,368 --> 00:04:02,119
In another, a run near the top of the scale reversed when I swapped in a min-risk oracle judge, setting agreement to minus one.

41
00:04:02,119 --> 00:04:03,578
Both are single experiments.

42
00:04:04,328 --> 00:04:10,496
The setup is two model families, small models, short runs, filtered supervised fine-tuning, and two narrow behaviors.

43
00:04:10,496 --> 00:04:19,567
As A I systems take over more of their own post-training, we need to know which feedback structures reinforce restraint and which amplify reward hacking.

44
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:25,373
Spread and agreement are two numbers you can measure in the first round and act on.
